  "metadata": {
    "title": "Recurring Decimals and Fraction Conversion",
    "description": "Recognize repeating decimals and derive reduced fractions for pure and mixed recurring decimals.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 40,
    "teachingApproach": "Recognize and notate a repeat → derive pure recurring conversion → handle prefixes and whole parts → verify and apply.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 3: recurring-decimal definitions and conversion rules on printed page 70; solved examples 17-18 on printed pages 72-73; objective patterns on recurring-to-fraction conversion in the chapter exercise and answer section."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A recurring decimal has a digit or block of digits that repeats forever. A bar over the repeating block marks exactly which digits recur. Before converting, identify whether repetition starts immediately after the decimal point or after a non-repeating prefix.\n\nThis distinction determines the denominator. A pure recurring block uses as many 9s as repeating digits. A mixed recurring decimal uses those 9s followed by as many zeroes as there are non-repeating digits. We will derive both rules so the denominator is meaningful rather than a memorized pattern.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "recognize_repeat"
        }
      ]
    },
    "recognize_repeat": {
      "id": "recognize_repeat",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A decimal is recurring when a digit or fixed block repeats without end. Parentheses mark the repeating part: 0.(3) means 0.3333…; 0.(27) means 0.272727… . If the written digits stop without a repeat mark, the decimal is terminating, such as 0.375. Do not infer that a finite printed string continues forever.\n\nA pure recurring decimal repeats immediately after the decimal point. A mixed recurring decimal has one or more non-repeating digits first, then a repeating block. The boundary between those portions controls the later conversion.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_recognize_repeat"
        }
      ]
    },
