    "derive_pure_single": {
      "id": "derive_pure_single",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Derive the pure recurring rule by shifting the decimal until the repeating tails line up. Let x = 0.(6) = 0.6666… . Then 10x = 6.6666… . Subtract the first equation from the second: 9x = 6, so x = 6/9 = 2/3. The infinite tails cancel because they are identical.\n\nFor a one-digit repeat, multiplication by 10 shifts one full period. Reduction to lowest terms is still required; 6/9 and 2/3 have the same value, but 2/3 is simplified.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_derive_pure_single"
        }
      ]
    },
