    "pure_multi_digit": {
      "id": "pure_multi_digit",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If a pure repeating block has several digits, multiply by the matching power of ten so one full block shifts past the point. For x = 0.(27) = 0.272727…, 100x = 27.272727… . Subtracting x leaves 99x = 27, so x = 27/99 = 3/11. Two repeating digits produce two 9s.\n\nFor a three-digit block, shifting three places produces 999x on subtraction. In general, a k-digit pure block is the integer made by the block divided by k nines, followed by reduction.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_pure_multi_digit"
        }
      ]
    },
